digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56722-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates whether mutant Huntingtin in Huntington's disease accelerates epigenetic aging in striatal neurons through altered PRC1 regulation and CBX paralog switching, leading to H3K27me3-associated de-repression of developmental genes and cellular identity loss that may help explain selective striatal neuron vulnerability.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A coherent paper-level proposal can be formed by combining the HD striatal vulnerability problem, the cell- and time-resolved epigenomic strategy, and cautious evidence implicating epigenetic aging, PRC1/CBX switching, developmental-gene de-repression, and identity loss."];
  "N_HD_Base" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently HD is a genetic disease (S1), and CAG expansion causes it (S2), so HD is defined as a genetic neurodegenerative disorder caused by HTT mutation."];
  "N_Method_Base" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently FANS used (S10), and models are profiled (S11), so the methodological base is established."];
  "N_Method_Time" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Currently methodological base is established (N_Method_Base), and temporal analysis is performed (S12), so the method covers temporal dynamics."];
  "N_Vuln_Prob" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently SPNs are vulnerable (S3), and molecular underpinning is elusive (S4), so there is a defined problem of unknown vulnerability mechanism."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The temporal FANS-based profiling strategy targets selected histone marks and PRC1-associated features, including H3K27me3, histone acetylation marks, and H2AK119ub, relevant to altered PRC1 regulation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The HD mutation accelerates loss of epigenetic information associated with cellular aging in striatal neurons."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Accelerated epigenetic aging in HD striatal neurons is associated with accelerated epigenetic de-repression of developmental genes involving PRC1 through CBX paralog switching."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Cell- and time-resolved analysis of histone-mark proxies can test whether epigenetic-aging-associated cellular identity loss occurs in vulnerable HD striatal neurons."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The evidence motivates the hypothesis that PRC1- and CBX-paralog-associated accelerated epigenetic aging, with de-repression of developmental genes, may contribute to cellular identity loss and striatal neuron vulnerability in Huntington's disease."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Huntington's disease provides the disease context in which striatal projection neuron vulnerability to the HD mutation and its elusive molecular underpinning form a defined vulnerability problem."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Epigenetic erosion or loss of epigenetic information links cellular aging with loss of cellular identity, rather than being strictly identical to cellular identity loss."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Histone modifications such as H3K27ac and H3K27me3 can be used as proxies to assess epigenetic-aging-associated cellular identity loss."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Prior bulk HD striatal evidence links epigenetic dysregulation, including loss of H3K27ac at super-enhancers, to downregulation of striatal identity genes within the HD vulnerability problem."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because prior bulk HD striatal evidence lacks sufficient cellular and temporal resolution, a key gap is whether vulnerable HD striatal neurons undergo accelerated epigenetic aging over disease progression."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) induction-case: Currently Huntington's disease is a genetic, progressive neurodegenerative disease with motor, cognitive, and psychiatric symptoms arising at midlife."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) induction-case: Currently we investigated HD striatal epigenetic landscape using FANS coupled to ChIPseq or CUT&Tag."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) induction-common: It is a common approach to profile neuronal and non-neuronal striatal cells in two reference HD mouse models (R6/1 and Q140)."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) induction-common: It is standard to perform temporal analysis at 3 ages corresponding to prodromal, early and advanced pathological stages."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) induction-common: It is consistent to target H3K27me3, histone acetylation marks, and H2AK119ub since analyses highlighted altered PRC1 regulation."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) deduction-case: Currently our data show that the HD mutation accelerates loss of epigenetic information associated with cellular aging in striatal neurons."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) induction-common: It is a common finding in our data that epigenetic de-repression of developmental genes is accelerated involving PRC1 through paralog switching."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) induction-common: It is a common fact that the disease is caused by an abnormal expansion of CAG trinucleotide repeats in the HTT gene producing mutant HTT."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) abduction-phenomenon: The phenomenon is that specific neurons (dSPN and iSPN) in the striatum are highly vulnerable to the HD mutation."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) abduction-knowledge: Existing knowledge indicates the molecular underpinning of striatal neuron vulnerability in HD remains elusive."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) deduction-rule: If epigenetic alteration occurs, then it is a key hallmark of cellular aging."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) deduction-case: Currently cellular aging is caused by loss of cellular identity resulting from erosion of epigenetic information (exdifferentiation)."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) deduction-case: Currently histone modifications (H3K27ac, H3K27me3) are used as proxy to assess exdifferentiation and cellular identity loss."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) deduction-rule: If bulk HD striatal tissue shows early loss of H3K27ac at super-enhancers, then it results in transcriptional downregulation of striatal identity genes."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) deduction-rule: If earlier analyses lack cellular and temporal resolutions, then it is unclear whether HD vulnerable neurons undergo accelerated epigenetic aging."];
  "S1" -> "N_HD_Base" [label="induction-case"];
